{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_498","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'Iteration 3|Iteration 4|Verdict|Completed three-format|Lesson' agent/journal.md | tail -n 30\"","aggregated_output":"11:**Verdict.** Keep as the fixed comparison baseline.\n13:**Lesson.** The validator scores several return intervals and a final absolute price; CRYPTO_1H additionally scores return gaps and realized volatility blocks. All changes must be evaluated by configuration.\n23:**Verdict.** Working candidate only; native-cadence validation pending.\n25:**Lesson.** Weekend variance reduction matters strongly for CRPS, especially XAU. A timestamp precision assumption silently eliminated all returns in the first implementation; use duration comparisons, not raw timestamp integers.\n50:**Verdict.** Keep recent blend for crypto 24h and 1h; keep fixed seasonality for COM_EQU. Confirm with a ten-day holdout before nomination.\n52:**Lesson.** After removing calendar effects, recent variance still contains useful information for crypto, especially for 1-hour scoring. Its effect is small relative to the calendar correction and cannot be inferred from one asset.\n54:## Iteration 3 — ten-day holdout (in progress)\n62:**Verdict.** Keep the calendar and recent-volatility rule. The ten-day configuration mean improves against the starter in all three formats; all 18 asset-format pairs improve.\n64:**Lesson.** A fixed calendar surface plus a recent level adjustment for crypto remained useful across the mixed ten-day September interval, not only the initial two-day screens.\n70:**Completed three-format ten-day result.** HYPE 1-hour finished, completing all 18 asset-format pairs. The unweighted mean CRPS across each configuration's active assets was:\n80:## Iteration 4 — heavy-tailed shocks (planned)\n90:**Verdict.** Keep Student-t(5) as the working 24-hour and 1-hour shock law. It improved all 18 asset-format pairs over the Gaussian rule on September 3–12, including WTIOIL 24-hour, SP500 24-hour, and HYPE 1-hour, which had tiny September 9–10 regressions. For this larger screen, predictions use synth-lib's float32 day-file layout; a BTC 1h 288-prompt parity check changed mean CRPS by only 0.00003 versus the JSON layout.\n92:**Lesson.** Matching variance is not sufficient for short-return CRPS; heavier conditional tails can improve both price and 1h volatility scoring. The benefit is materially larger in the quiet weekend sample than in the active September sample.\n100:**Verdict.** Pending; reject if configuration-wide mean CRPS regresses in either window or if any large asset regression outweighs small gains.\n112:**Verdict.** Pending; keep only if configuration-wide CRPS improves consistently and outliers do not create material asset regressions.\n124:**Verdict.** Pending. Advance only if the five-asset mean 1-hour CRPS improves in both short windows without an asset regression above 1%.\n128:**Verdict.** Discard the all-week regression under the pre-registered consistency rule. The observed weekend/weekday split motivates a separate, explicitly conditional test below; it is not evidence that the all-week rule works.\n136:**Verdict.** Pending.\n144:**Verdict.** Pending.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
