{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_505","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n -C 3 'Short-screen result|Iteration 8|Verdict.\\\\*\\\\* Pending' agent/journal.md\"","aggregated_output":"97-\n98-**Change.** Keep the calendar factors, volatility estimator, Student-t(5) shocks, and seeding. Multiply each path's shock scales by `exp(0.3 Z - 0.3²)`, where `Z` is one standard normal draw for that path. This keeps the mean squared multiplier at one. Test on both scored-date windows against Student-t(5) without the multiplier before considering a longer holdout.\n99-\n100:**Verdict.** Pending; reject if configuration-wide mean CRPS regresses in either window or if any large asset regression outweighs small gains.\n101-\n102-**Paired-draw correction.** The initial implementation drew path-scale multipliers before the Student-t shocks, changing the random shock stream relative to the Student-t(5) baseline. Its August gains and mixed September 24-hour result are exploratory because Monte Carlo noise is not controlled within the pair. A follow-up `t5_mix_crn.py` draws the Student-t shocks first, then the multipliers, so the shock array matches the baseline exactly. The first follow-up focuses on BTC and ETH 1-hour, where the initial variant improved both windows.\n103-\n--\n109-\n110-**Change.** Retain the selected variance model, independent path shocks, and seeding. Change only the Student-t degrees of freedom from five to three, with variance normalization `sqrt(1/3)`. Compare against Student-t(5) on the two exact-prompt windows before any ten-day confirmation.\n111-\n112:**Verdict.** Pending; keep only if configuration-wide CRPS improves consistently and outliers do not create material asset regressions.\n113-\n114-**Screen extension.** BTC and ETH 1-hour together improve under Student-t(3) in both short windows, though September BTC alone regresses by 0.09%. Test SOL, XRP, and HYPE 1-hour on the same two windows before a configuration-wide verdict. The 24-hour sample shows September regressions for BTC, XAU, and WTIOIL, so this candidate is now primarily an evaluation of 1-hour shocks.\n115-\n--\n121-\n122-**Change.** Fit one small log-variance regression per crypto asset on the frozen January–July 2026 minute data, using only targets whose next hour ends by July 31. Preserve the Student-t(5) shocks, calendar profile, seeding, and 24-hour behavior. Replace only the predicted variance level for 1-hour prompts. Compare at 1000 paths on both exact-prompt short windows before an independent holdout.\n123-\n124:**Verdict.** Pending. Advance only if the five-asset mean 1-hour CRPS improves in both short windows without an asset regression above 1%.\n125-\n126-**Result.** The fitted hourly regression used 9,797–9,838 January–July training origins per crypto asset. On August 15–16, it lowered the five-asset CRYPTO_1H mean CRPS from Student-t(5)'s 311.96 to 304.49 (2.40%); all five assets improved. On September 9–10, it raised the mean from 864.43 to 865.07 (0.07%); BTC, ETH, SOL, and XRP regressed slightly, while HYPE improved. All runs used exact starts, 1000 paths, and full realized coverage.\n127-\n--\n133-\n134-**Change.** Use the July-trained HAR level only for weekend CRYPTO_1H starts, otherwise use Student-t(5)'s existing volatility estimate. Preserve the same shocks, calendar factors, and seed. Evaluate on the nonoverlapping August 20–29 scored dates, which include weekend and weekday starts, against Student-t(5) for all five assets at 1000 paths. If the five-asset mean improves and no asset regresses above 1%, confirm on September 1–8, which also contains a weekend and does not overlap the September 9–10 screen.\n135-\n136:**Verdict.** Pending.\n137-\n138:**Short-screen result.** On September 9–10, the eight-asset COM_EQU_24H mean CRPS moved from 2402.94 with the July profile to 2396.84 with the August 31 profile (0.25% lower). Seven assets improved; NVDAX regressed by 0.04%, below the stated 1% cutoff. SPCX had the largest gain, 4065.71 → 4029.84 (0.88%). This passes the screen for the nonoverlapping September 1–8 check.\n139-\n140:## Iteration 8 — newer calendar fit for COM_EQU (planned)\n141-\n142-**Hypothesis.** SPCX and SP500 had relatively little history in the July 31 calendar fit. Adding the complete August month may reduce hourly-profile noise and lower September COM_EQU_24H CRPS.\n143-\n144-**Change.** Refit the same per-asset hour-by-weekend profile through August 31, retaining every other model choice from Student-t(5). Screen all eight COM_EQU assets on September 9–10 at exact prompts and 1000 paths, then compare on the nonoverlapping September 1–8 scored dates if the short screen improves the eight-asset mean without an asset regression above 1%.\n145-\n146:**Verdict.** Pending.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_506","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '116,155p' agent/journal.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_506","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '116,155p' agent/journal.md\"","aggregated_output":"**Full short-screen result.** For CRYPTO_1H, Student-t(3) versus Student-t(5) averaged 307.46 versus 311.96 across five assets on August 15–16 (better 1.44%), but 864.75 versus 864.43 on September 9–10 (worse 0.04%). HYPE's September CRPS rose from 1211.58 to 1217.98, and BTC rose from 567.02 to 567.55. ETH, SOL, and XRP each improved in both windows. Student-t(3) therefore fails the pre-registered configuration-wide consistency rule; test only those three asset-specific possibilities on the independent August 20–29 holdout before deciding whether exceptions are justified. The sampled 24-hour assets also all regressed in September and are rejected for that horizon.\n\n## Iteration 7 — multi-horizon variance forecast (planned)\n\n**Hypothesis.** The current half-blend of seven-day and 24-hour realized variance may miss shorter volatility changes relevant to CRYPTO_1H. A regularized regression on past 1-hour, 6-hour, 24-hour, and 7-day calendar-adjusted variance may lower CRPS, especially its realized-volatility component.\n\n**Change.** Fit one small log-variance regression per crypto asset on the frozen January–July 2026 minute data, using only targets whose next hour ends by July 31. Preserve the Student-t(5) shocks, calendar profile, seeding, and 24-hour behavior. Replace only the predicted variance level for 1-hour prompts. Compare at 1000 paths on both exact-prompt short windows before an independent holdout.\n\n**Verdict.** Pending. Advance only if the five-asset mean 1-hour CRPS improves in both short windows without an asset regression above 1%.\n\n**Result.** The fitted hourly regression used 9,797–9,838 January–July training origins per crypto asset. On August 15–16, it lowered the five-asset CRYPTO_1H mean CRPS from Student-t(5)'s 311.96 to 304.49 (2.40%); all five assets improved. On September 9–10, it raised the mean from 864.43 to 865.07 (0.07%); BTC, ETH, SOL, and XRP regressed slightly, while HYPE improved. All runs used exact starts, 1000 paths, and full realized coverage.\n\n**Verdict.** Discard the all-week regression under the pre-registered consistency rule. The observed weekend/weekday split motivates a separate, explicitly conditional test below; it is not evidence that the all-week rule works.\n\n## Iteration 9 — weekend-only hourly regression (planned)\n\n**Hypothesis.** The HAR variance forecast's August benefit may reflect quieter weekends; applying it only to crypto 1-hour prompts starting on Saturday or Sunday may retain that benefit without its September weekday regression.\n\n**Change.** Use the July-trained HAR level only for weekend CRYPTO_1H starts, otherwise use Student-t(5)'s existing volatility estimate. Preserve the same shocks, calendar factors, and seed. Evaluate on the nonoverlapping August 20–29 scored dates, which include weekend and weekday starts, against Student-t(5) for all five assets at 1000 paths. If the five-asset mean improves and no asset regresses above 1%, confirm on September 1–8, which also contains a weekend and does not overlap the September 9–10 screen.\n\n**Verdict.** Pending.\n\n**Short-screen result.** On September 9–10, the eight-asset COM_EQU_24H mean CRPS moved from 2402.94 with the July profile to 2396.84 with the August 31 profile (0.25% lower). Seven assets improved; NVDAX regressed by 0.04%, below the stated 1% cutoff. SPCX had the largest gain, 4065.71 → 4029.84 (0.88%). This passes the screen for the nonoverlapping September 1–8 check.\n\n## Iteration 8 — newer calendar fit for COM_EQU (planned)\n\n**Hypothesis.** SPCX and SP500 had relatively little history in the July 31 calendar fit. Adding the complete August month may reduce hourly-profile noise and lower September COM_EQU_24H CRPS.\n\n**Change.** Refit the same per-asset hour-by-weekend profile through August 31, retaining every other model choice from Student-t(5). Screen all eight COM_EQU assets on September 9–10 at exact prompts and 1000 paths, then compare on the nonoverlapping September 1–8 scored dates if the short screen improves the eight-asset mean without an asset regression above 1%.\n\n**Verdict.** Pending.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
