{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_517","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n -C 4 'Iteration 10|Iteration 9|Iteration 8' agent/journal.md\"","aggregated_output":"126-**Result.** The fitted hourly regression used 9,797–9,838 January–July training origins per crypto asset. On August 15–16, it lowered the five-asset CRYPTO_1H mean CRPS from Student-t(5)'s 311.96 to 304.49 (2.40%); all five assets improved. On September 9–10, it raised the mean from 864.43 to 865.07 (0.07%); BTC, ETH, SOL, and XRP regressed slightly, while HYPE improved. All runs used exact starts, 1000 paths, and full realized coverage.\n127-\n128-**Verdict.** Discard the all-week regression under the pre-registered consistency rule. The observed weekend/weekday split motivates a separate, explicitly conditional test below; it is not evidence that the all-week rule works.\n129-\n130:## Iteration 8 — newer calendar fit for COM_EQU (planned)\n131-\n132-**Hypothesis.** SPCX and SP500 had relatively little history in the July 31 calendar fit. Adding the complete August month may reduce hourly-profile noise and lower September COM_EQU_24H CRPS.\n133-\n134-**Change.** Refit the same per-asset hour-by-weekend profile through August 31, retaining every other model choice from Student-t(5). Screen all eight COM_EQU assets on September 9–10 at exact prompts and 1000 paths, then compare on the nonoverlapping September 1–8 scored dates if the short screen improves the eight-asset mean without an asset regression above 1%.\n--\n136-**Short-screen result.** On September 9–10, the eight-asset COM_EQU_24H mean CRPS moved from 2402.94 with the July profile to 2396.84 with the August 31 profile (0.25% lower). Seven assets improved; NVDAX regressed by 0.04%, below the stated 1% cutoff. SPCX had the largest gain, 4065.71 → 4029.84 (0.88%). This passes the screen for the nonoverlapping September 1–8 check.\n137-\n138-**Verdict.** Pending.\n139-\n140:## Iteration 10 — selected 1-hour shock laws (planned)\n141-\n142-**Hypothesis.** Asset-specific 1-hour shocks supported by two short screens and the separate August 20–29 holdout may lower configuration-wide CRPS on a new September period.\n143-\n144-**Change.** Keep Student-t(5) for every 24-hour asset and HYPE 1-hour. For BTC 1-hour, use Student-t(5) plus the paired-draw pathwise lognormal scale with log-standard-deviation 0.3; for ETH, SOL, and XRP 1-hour, use unit-variance Student-t(3). All other variance and calendar calculations remain as in the baseline. Score September 1–8 exact starts at 1000 paths against Student-t(5) for all five crypto assets.\n145-\n146-**Decision rule.** Keep the hybrid if its five-asset mean CRPS improves and no asset regresses above 1%; otherwise retain the robust Student-t(5) baseline or only independently supported per-asset exceptions.\n147-\n148:## Iteration 9 — weekend-only hourly regression (planned)\n149-\n150-**Hypothesis.** The HAR variance forecast's August benefit may reflect quieter weekends; applying it only to crypto 1-hour prompts starting on Saturday or Sunday may retain that benefit without its September weekday regression.\n151-\n152-**Change.** Use the July-trained HAR level only for weekend CRYPTO_1H starts, otherwise use Student-t(5)'s existing volatility estimate. Preserve the same shocks, calendar factors, and seed. Evaluate on the nonoverlapping August 20–29 scored dates, which include weekend and weekday starts, against Student-t(5) for all five assets at 1000 paths. If the five-asset mean improves and no asset regresses above 1%, confirm on September 1–8, which also contains a weekend and does not overlap the September 9–10 screen.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
