{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_603","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat agent/report.md'","aggregated_output":"# Calendar volatility and price-fan calibration for Synth\n\n## Context\n\nSynth scores simulated price fans with the validator's CRPS. Lower CRPS is better. The current configurations are CRYPTO_24H, COM_EQU_24H, and CRYPTO_1H; the last also scores realized-volatility blocks. All results here use the frozen venue-matched minute snapshot and the official `synth_lib.backtester` scorer. The data cutoff is 2026-09-14.\n\n## Approach\n\nThe starter was a Gaussian log-price random walk with one volatility estimate from the preceding seven days. I fit a per-asset relative minute-variance profile by UTC hour and weekday/weekend using adjacent valid closes through 2026-07-31. Weekly normalization removes broad volatility-regime changes before averaging calendar cells; neighboring hours are smoothed and the result is shrunk toward each weekday/weekend mean. At each prompt, the model estimates the current variance level from the preceding seven-day OHLCV frame after dividing valid adjacent squared log returns by the fitted calendar factor. It projects that factor across every future minute, then sums minute variances within the requested output step. Crypto also blends the seven-day variance level equally with a 24-hour-half-life estimate. Simulated log returns have zero drift; every path starts at the last valid context close.\n\nThe model respects `time_increment`, `time_length`, and `num_simulations`. It handles NaN minutes without interpolation and seeds its NumPy generator from `SYNTH_BENCHMARK_SEED` and the prompt identity. The serialized calendar profile is included in the repository, so serving needs no network access.\n\n## Experiments\n\nThe first evaluator used a regular hourly prompt grid. Historical prompt starts actually have irregular minute offsets, and synth-lib's matching tolerance can pair a prompt with a later prediction. Those early CRPS figures were discarded. The corrected evaluator queries the offline score cache for exact prompt starts and verifies snapshot coverage through the full forecast horizon. A separate implementation bug compared raw timestamp integers as if they were nanoseconds; on the pandas timestamp unit in this environment, it excluded every adjacent return and made the fan far too wide. The journal records both failed attempts and their scores.\n\nOn two scored-date windows, August 15–16 and September 9–10, every active COM_EQU asset improved with fixed calendar seasonality relative to the starter. The average per-asset CRPS moved from 1613.31 to 1260.84 on the August weekend and from 2523.42 to 2411.60 in September. Across five crypto assets, the starter, fixed-calendar, and recent-volatility models averaged 1697.79, 1582.61, and 1572.80 for 24-hour scoring in August; 3862.87, 3847.72, and 3831.82 in September. For 1-hour scoring the corresponding figures were 349.90, 319.37, and 315.47 in August; 873.12, 868.41, and 866.21 in September. Each asset had 48 prompts per 24-hour format or 288 per 1-hour format, with 1000 paths and full realized-price coverage.\n\nThe recent-volatility effect is small next to the calendar effect. It improved fixed seasonality for every crypto 1-hour asset in both windows, while BTC 24-hour on the August weekend regressed by 0.5%. The selected working rule uses the recent blend for crypto and fixed calendar variance for COM_EQU. A unit-variance Student-t(5) shock variant is being tested separately after a training-window diagnostic found heavy standardized minute-return tails.\n\n## Results\n\nTen-day, all-asset official backtests are in progress. Final configuration CRPS and per-asset comparisons will be added before nomination.\n\n## Limitations\n\nThe static calendar profile is fitted only through July 31 to preserve forward validation on the August and September windows. A newer listing has a shorter, thinner training history. The model uses closes for its variance estimate and does not model jumps, drift, cross-asset information, or uncertainty in the volatility level beyond the fan's return shocks. The short-window tests include very different volatility regimes; a small percentage gain there may not persist. The ten-day holdout and the 10 days after the cutoff remain distinct, so backtest CRPS is evidence rather than a guarantee of future score.\n\n## With more budget\n\nI would test stochastic volatility and heavy-tailed shocks on more independent forward windows, fit the calendar surface with recency weights once enough history accumulates for newer listings, and compare a direct CRPS-trained volatility forecaster against the simple seven-day/24-hour blend. Exact prompt matching and stored per-prompt scores would make those comparisons more reliable.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
