{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_606","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"git diff --stat agent/report.md && rg -n 'Student-t|backtester|Results|Limitations' agent/report.md\"","aggregated_output":" agent/report.md | 22 ++++++++++++++++------\n 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)\n5:Synth scores simulated price fans with the validator's CRPS. Lower CRPS is better. The current configurations are CRYPTO_24H, COM_EQU_24H, and CRYPTO_1H; the last also scores realized-volatility blocks. All results here use the frozen venue-matched minute snapshot and the official `synth_lib.backtester` scorer. The data cutoff is 2026-09-14.\n11:Independent simulated step returns use unit-variance Student-t(5) shocks. The model respects `time_increment`, `time_length`, and `num_simulations`. It handles NaN minutes without interpolation and seeds its NumPy generator from `SYNTH_BENCHMARK_SEED` and the prompt identity. The serialized calendar profile is included in the repository, so serving needs no network access.\n15:The first evaluator used a regular hourly prompt grid. Historical prompt starts actually have irregular minute offsets, and synth-lib's matching tolerance can pair a prompt with a later prediction. Those early CRPS figures were discarded. The corrected evaluator queries the offline score cache for exact prompt starts and checks snapshot coverage through the backtester's 26-hour price-read buffer. A separate implementation bug compared raw timestamp integers as if they were nanoseconds; on the pandas timestamp unit in this environment, it excluded every adjacent return and made the fan far too wide. The journal records both failed attempts and their scores.\n19:The recent-volatility effect is small next to the calendar effect. It improved fixed seasonality for every crypto 1-hour asset in both windows, while BTC 24-hour on the August weekend regressed by 0.5%. The working rule uses the recent blend for crypto and fixed calendar variance for COM_EQU. Standardized one-minute returns had heavy tails in the January–July fit period: their 99.9th absolute percentiles were 6.39 for BTC, 6.69 for ETH, and 7.80 for XAU, versus 3.29 for a standard normal. Student-t(5) shocks improved all 18 asset-format pairs over Gaussian shocks in the ten-day September test.\n21:Heavier Student-t(3) shocks improved the quiet August weekend, but raised the September 9–10 COM_EQU CRPS for every sampled asset and slightly raised the five-asset crypto 1-hour mean. Student-t(4) improved the August BTC/XAU sample but its September gain was only about 0.05%, below the predeclared expansion threshold. A four-window regression using past 1-hour, 6-hour, 24-hour, and 7-day variance lowered August 1-hour CRPS, then raised the September weekday mean; the all-week version was discarded. Separate weekend-only and asset-specific variants are described in the journal and are being confirmed on nonoverlapping dates.\n23:## Results\n27:| Configuration | Starter | Calendar Gaussian | Student-t(5) |\n33:All 18 Student-t(5) asset-format CRPS values were below their matched Gaussian and starter values. The finalized serving model's configuration CRPS will be reported after its current confirmations finish.\n35:## Limitations\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
