er 3–12 confirmation.\nagent/journal.md:358:**Decision rule.** Advance only if NVDAX improves on all three two-day windows; reject on any regression. If it passes, keep only if the ten-day September 3–12 score also improves.\nagent/journal.md:362:**Verdict.** Discard the NVDAX-specific recent blend. The September 1–2 gain did not carry to the weekend; keep the selected seven-day variance level.\nagent/journal.md:366:## Iteration 22 — half-hour weekday COM_EQU calendar (planned)\nagent/journal.md:368:**Hypothesis.** The hourly COM_EQU calendar profile blurs sharp volatility changes around US market openings. A 30-minute weekday profile, fitted by the same weekly-normalized squared-return procedure through August 31, may improve 5-minute path CRPS while preserving the established weekend profile and each asset's weekday mean variance.\nagent/journal.md:370:**Change.** Fit 48 weekday time-of-day bins for each of eight COM_EQU assets, using the same 99.9th-percentile return cap, adjacent valid closes, `[1,2,1]/4` circular smoothing, and 15% shrinkage as the selected hourly fit. Rescale each new weekday profile to the mean of its existing August 31 hourly weekday profile; use the existing weekend factors unchanged. Crypto profiles, variance levels, shocks, and seeds remain unchanged. Screen all eight COM_EQU 24-hour assets on September 1–2, then September 5–6, then September 9–10 at exact prompts and 1000 paths.\nagent/journal.md:372:**Decision rule.** Advance only if the eight-asset mean improves in each of the three windows and no asset regresses more than 1% on any. Stop at the first failed window. If it passes, confirm on September 3–12 before any serving change.\nagent/journal.md:374:**First weekday screen.** On September 1–2, the eight-asset mean fell from 2329.87 with the selected hourly profile to 2313.99 with weekday half-hour bins (0.68%). All eight assets improved, by 0.07–1.36%, on 48 exact prompts each with 1000 paths and full coverage. The fitted weekday profiles peaked at 13:30 UTC for seven assets and 14:00 for SP500, supporting the market-opening mechanism. This passes the first gate; test the weekend next.\nagent/journal.md:378:**Second weekday result.** On September 9–10, the eight-asset mean fell from 2396.84 to 2389.61 (0.30%). Six assets improved; GOOGLX and WTIOIL regressed by less than 0.01%. Each pair had 48 exact prompts, 1000 paths, and full coverage. All three short-window gates pass, so confirm on September 3–12 before serving.\nagent/journal.md:380:**Ten-day confirmation.** On September 3–12, the eight-asset mean fell from 1953.75 to 1947.43 (0.32%). Seven assets improved by 0.06–0.55%; SP500 rose by 0.01%. Each candidate run had 240 exact prompts, 1000 paths, and full realized coverage. The mean improved in all four tested September windows.\nagent/journal.md:382:**Verdict.** Keep the half-hour weekday profile for COM_EQU 24-hour, retaining the hourly weekend profile and crypto calendars. Apply the profile to `modeling.py`, then verify candidate parity and direct serving-module CRPS.\nagent/journal.md:384:**Serving verification.** Sampled weekday and weekend output paths from `modeling.py` exactly matched the scored candidate for all eight COM_EQU assets. A direct official September 3–12 backtest of the updated serving module reproduced all eight candidate asset CRPS values exactly, with 240 exact prompts, 1000 paths, and full coverage each. The verified eight-asset mean was 1947.43. Crypto branches were not changed by this iteration.\nagent/journal.md:386:**Lesson.** Preserving each asset's weekday average variance while resolving the opening period more finely improved most COM_EQU assets. This supports time-of-day shape as a useful calibration dimension beyond the broad weekday/weekend split.\nagent/journal.md:388:## Iteration 23 — quarter-hour weekday COM_EQU calendar (planned)\nagent/journal.md:392:**Change.** Fit 96 weekday bins through August 31 with the same adjacent-return filter, weekly normalization, 99.9th-percentile cap, circular `[1,2,1]/4` smoothing, and 15% shrinkage used for the selected 30-minute profile. Rescale each asset's weekday mean to the selected profile's mean and leave its weekend profile unchanged. Compare all eight COM_EQU 24-hour assets at exact September 1–2 prompts and 1000 paths. If that passes, check September 5–6, September 9–10, and September 3–12 in sequence.\nagent/journal.md:394:**Decision rule.** Advance only if eight-asset mean CRPS improves on each window and no asset regresses more than 1%. Stop after the first failed window; otherwise replace only the COM_EQU weekday profile.\nagent/journal.md:400:**Second weekday result.** On September 9–10, the eight-asset mean fell from 2389.61 to 2385.58 (0.17%). All eight assets improved, by 0.06–0.49%, with 48 exact prompts, 1000 paths, and full coverage. All three short-window gates pass; confirm on September 3–12 before serving.\nagent/journal.md:402:**Ten-day confirmation.** On September 3–12, the eight-asset mean fell from 1947.43 with 30-minute bins to 1944.22 with 15-minute bins (0.16%). Seven assets improved by 0.10–0.32%; WTIOIL rose by 0.05%, below the 1% limit. Every asset had 240 exact prompts, 1000 paths, and full coverage. The mean improved in all four tested windows.\nagent/journal.md:404:**Verdict.** Keep the quarter-hour weekday COM_EQU profile, with the same hourly weekend factors and other model components. Verify sampled path parity and direct serving-module scores before considering it consolidated.\nagent/journal.md:406:**Serving verification.** Sampled weekday and weekend paths from `modeling.py` matched the scored quarter-hour candidate for all eight COM_EQU assets. A direct official September 3–12 backtest of the updated serving module reproduced all eight candidate asset CRPS values exactly, with 240 exact prompts, 1000 paths, and full coverage each. The verified eight-asset mean was 1944.22. Crypto serving logic was unchanged.\nagent/journal.md:410:## Iteration 24 — five-minute weekday COM_EQU calendar (planned)\nagent/journal.md:412:**Hypothesis.** Even the selected 15-minute profile may average away the unusually volatile first five minutes after the US cash opening. A 5-minute weekday profile could better calibrate the validator's 5-minute path increments.\nagent/journal.md:414:**Change.** Fit 288 weekday time-of-day bins through August 31 using the selected profile's return filter, weekly normalization, 99.9th-percentile cap, circular `[1,2,1]/4` smoothing, and 15% shrinkage. Rescale each asset's new weekday factors to the selected 15-minute profile's mean; keep the hourly weekend factors, variance level, shocks, and seeds unchanged. Screen all eight COM_EQU assets on September 1–2 at exact prompts and 1000 paths. If it passes, test September 5–6, September 9–10, and September 3–12 in that order.\nagent/journal.md:416:**Decision rule.** Advance only if the eight-asset mean CRPS improves on each window and no asset regresses more than 1%. Stop after the first failed window; otherwise replace only the COM_EQU weekday profile.\nagent/journal.md:422:**Second weekday result.** On September 9–10, the eight-asset mean fell from 2385.58 to 2381.50 (0.17%). All eight assets improved, by 0.04–0.45%, on 48 exact prompts each with 1000 paths and full coverage. All three short-window gates pass; confirm on September 3–12 before serving.\nagent/report.md:5:Synth scores simulated price fans with the validator's CRPS. Lower CRPS is better. The current configurations are CRYPTO_24H, COM_EQU_24H, and CRYPTO_1H; the last also scores realized-volatility blocks. All results here use the frozen venue-matched minute snapshot and the official `synth_lib.backtester` scorer. The data cutoff is 2026-09-14.\nagent/report.md:11:Independent simulated step returns use unit-variance Student-t(5) shocks for every 24-hour format and for HYPE 1-hour. ETH, SOL, and XRP 1-hour use Student-t(3); BTC 1-hour uses Student-t(5) with a pathwise lognormal volatility multiplier. On crypto 1-hour weekend prompts, a July-trained regression of recent 1-hour, 6-hour, 24-hour, and 7-day adjusted variance replaces the usual volatility level. HYPE 1-hour uses that regression on weekdays as well. Crypto 24-hour applies a 0.70 factor to predicted variance for weekend starts and 0.85 for weekday starts. The eight COM_EQU assets use a calendar profile fitted through August 31: 15-minute weekday bins and the original hourly weekend bins. Crypto retains the July hourly profile. The model respects `time_increment`, `time_length`, and `num_simulations`. It handles NaN minutes without interpolation and seeds its NumPy generator from `SYNTH_BENCHMARK_SEED` and the prompt identity. The serialized profiles and regression coefficients are included in the repository, so serving needs no network access.\nagent/report.md:17:On two scored-date windows, August 15–16 and September 9–10, every active COM_EQU asset improved with fixed calendar seasonality relative to the starter. The average per-asset CRPS moved from 1613.31 to 1260.84 on the August weekend and from 2523.42 to 2411.60 in September. Across five crypto assets, the starter, fixed-calendar, and recent-volatility models averaged 1697.79, 1582.61, and 1572.80 for 24-hour scoring in August; 3862.87, 3847.72, and 3831.82 in September. For 1-hour scoring the corresponding figures were 349.90, 319.37, and 315.47 in August; 873.12, 868.41, and 866.21 in September. Each asset had 48 prompts per 24-hour format or 288 per 1-hour format, with 1000 paths and full realized-price coverage.\nagent/report.md:19:The recent-volatility effect is small next to the calendar effect. It improved fixed seasonality for every crypto 1-hour asset in both windows, while BTC 24-hour on the August weekend regressed by 0.5%. The working rule uses the recent blend for crypto and fixed calendar variance for COM_EQU. Standardized one-minute returns had heavy tails in the January–July fit period: their 99.9th absolute percentiles were 6.39 for BTC, 6.69 for ETH, and 7.80 for XAU, versus 3.29 for a standard normal. Student-t(5) shocks improved all 18 asset-format pairs over Gaussian shocks in the ten-day September test.\nagent/report.md:21:Heavier Student-t(3) shocks improved the quiet August weekend, but raised the September 9–10 COM_EQU CRPS for every sampled asset and slightly raised the five-asset crypto 1-hour mean. Independent checks supported Student-t(3) for ETH, SOL, and XRP 1-hour only. Student-t(4) improved the August BTC/XAU sample but its September gain was only about 0.05%, below the predeclared expansion threshold. A four-window regression using past 1-hour, 6-hour, 24-hour, and 7-day variance lowered August 1-hour CRPS, then raised the September weekday mean; the all-week version was discarded. Applying it only on weekends improved five-asset 1-hour CRPS on both August 20–29 and September 1–8. Combined with the selected 1-hour shock laws, it improved the September 3–12 mean from 803.14 to 799.89. Refitting the COM_EQU calendar through August 31 improved all eight assets on September 1–8 and September 3–12.\nagent/report.md:23:A final test multiplied the predicted variance by 0.85. This improved all five crypto 24-hour assets on the independent August 5–14 holdout and lowered their mean by 0.31%; on September 3–12 it lowered the mean by 0.28%, with a 0.02% HYPE regression. The same factor raised crypto 1-hour CRPS on September 9–10 by 0.16% and COM_EQU 24-hour CRPS by 0.13%, so those formats retain their earlier variance rules. A stronger 0.70 crypto 24-hour factor improved the August weekend but raised September 9–10 mean CRPS by 0.27%, so it was rejected.\nagent/report.md:25:Applying 0.70 only on weekend crypto 24-hour starts improved three mixed windows over 0.85 throughout: September 3–12 by 0.05%, August 5–14 by 0.04%, and the independent August 20–29 window by 0.23%. All five assets improved on the last window, so the conditional rule replaced the constant 0.85 factor.\nagent/report.md:29:Adding the crypto-style recent variance blend to COM_EQU improved its September 1–2 mean by 0.62%, mostly through NVDAX, but raised XAU's September 5–6 weekend CRPS by 1.51%. The configuration-wide blend failed its predeclared asset limit and was discarded.\nagent/report.md:32:The selected hourly COM_EQU profile smoothed the cash opening spike across neighboring hours. Fitting 30-minute weekday bins through August 31, while preserving the old weekday mean and weekend factors, placed the peak at 13:30 UTC for seven assets and 14:00 for SP500. The eight-asset mean improved by 0.68% on September 1–2, 0.28% on September 5–6, 0.30% on September 9–10, and 0.32% on September 3–12.\nagent/report.md:33:Fitting 15-minute weekday bins sharpened that opening profile further. Relative to the 30-minute version, it improved all eight assets on both two-day weekday screens and the weekend screen, and lowered the September 3–12 mean by 0.16%; WTIOIL alone rose by 0.05% on the ten-day window.\nagent/report.md:35:An asset-specific revision to the earlier all-week HAR failure succeeded: using the hourly variance regression on HYPE weekdays lowered HYPE 1-hour CRPS by 0.59% on September 1–8, 1.33% on August 20–29, and 0.42% on September 3–12. The other four crypto 1-hour assets keep the weekend-only rule.\nagent/report.md:39:The official September 3–12 backtest used 1000 paths per prompt, 240 exact starts per 24-hour asset, 1440 per 1-hour asset, and full realized coverage. The table shows the unweighted mean of each asset's prompt-level CRPS within its configuration. Lower is better.\nagent/report.md:43:| COM_EQU_24H (8 assets) | 2176.07 | 1981.98 | 1962.43 | 1944.22 |\nagent/report.md:47:All 18 Student-t(5) asset-format CRPS values were below their matched Gaussian and starter values. The initial selected module produced all 18 expected asset-format results with exact prompt counts and 100% realized coverage. A crypto 24-hour variance adjustment passed a separate August 5–14 holdout (2510.26 → 2502.36) and the September 3–12 confirmation (3797.67 → 3787.20). The selected weekend refinement further lowered September 3–12 crypto 24-hour CRPS to 3785.15 and passed an independent August 20–29 confirmation. Direct serving-module backtests reproduced all five final crypto 24-hour candidate scores exactly, with 240 prompts and full coverage per asset. A uniform adjustment to crypto 1-hour and COM_EQU was rejected after September 9–10 regressions; HYPE's weekday HAR exception improved on two longer windows, and its direct serving backtest reproduced the candidate's 1054.19 CRPS. The finer COM_EQU weekday profiles passed three short screens and the full September confirmation; direct serving backtests reproduced all eight final quarter-hour candidate scores exactly. The selected model's gain over the starter is 10.66% for COM_EQU_24H, 3.02% for CRYPTO_24H, and 3.86% for CRYPTO_1H.\nagent/report.md:51:The July calendar profile preserves forward validation on both August and September windows; the COM_EQU refit through August 31 has only September validation. Newer listings have shorter, thinner training histories. The model uses closes for its variance estimate and does not model drift or cross-asset information. Only BTC 1-hour has explicit pathwise volatility uncertainty. The short-window tests include different volatility regimes, and a small percentage gain may not persist. Every backtest is before the September 14 data cutoff; the ten days after that cutoff remain unseen.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
